course: history
lesson: 14
title: Rome: the empire, the legions and the roads
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: At its biggest, about 1,900 years ago, the Roman Empire stretched from Britain to Egypt and from Spain to Syria, all around the Mediterranean, with many peoples living under one law and one money.
explain: Under emperors, everything depended on one person: good ones like Augustus, Trajan and Marcus Aurelius ruled well, while cruel or foolish ones like Caligula and Nero did much harm.
explain: Rome held this empire together with a professional army, the legions, and with straight paved roads. It is like a giant train network: the faster soldiers and messages can travel, the easier a huge country is to run.
explain: Romans were great engineers, building aqueducts for water, arches, domes and concrete. But not everyone welcomed them: Boudica in Britain led a great revolt, and Hadrian built a wall to mark where the empire ended.
fact: At its biggest, about 1,900 years ago, the Roman Empire stretched from Britain to Egypt and from Spain to Syria, around the whole Mediterranean.
fact: Some emperors, like Augustus, Trajan and Marcus Aurelius, ruled well; others, like Caligula and Nero, were cruel or foolish.
fact: The story that Nero played the fiddle while Rome burned is a myth; the fiddle had not even been invented.
fact: The Roman army was made of legions of about 5,000 well-trained professional soldiers with armour, shields and short swords; they won more by discipline, training and teamwork than by having more men.
fact: Legionaries marched about 30 kilometres a day carrying their kit, built a fortified camp every night and served for 25 years; at the end they got land or money, and many settled in the provinces, the lands of the empire outside Italy.
fact: In the testudo, or tortoise formation, soldiers locked their shields above and around them to protect against arrows.
fact: The Romans built about 80,000 kilometres of straight, paved roads so armies and messengers could move fast, and many modern roads still follow Roman routes.
fact: Aqueducts carried fresh water from hills to cities, sometimes over high stone bridges, using a gentle downhill slope.
fact: The Romans used arches, domes and concrete, and the Pantheon in Rome, nearly 1,900 years old, still has the world's largest unreinforced concrete dome.
fact: Emperor Hadrian built a wall about 117 kilometres long across northern Britain to mark the empire's edge and control movement.
fact: Boudica, queen of the Iceni in Britain, led a big revolt after the Romans treated her family and people badly; she burned Londinium but was finally defeated, and Britain remembers her as a symbol of courage against injustice.
fact: Roman soldiers and traders came from all over the empire, and some soldiers on Hadrian's Wall came from Iraq and North Africa.
exercise: How far did the Roman Empire stretch at its biggest? | From Britain to Egypt and from Spain to Syria | From China all the way to the south of India | Only across Italy and a few islands | From Norway to Russia
exercise: Which emperor was known as a good ruler? | Marcus Aurelius | Caligula | Nero | Attila
exercise: Why is it a danger when one emperor rules everything? | Everything depends on one person being good | Emperors could never make any decisions alone | Too many people get a vote | The army would simply vanish
exercise: About how many soldiers were in a legion? | About 5,000 | About 50 | About 500,000 | About 5
exercise: What made the Roman army so strong? | Discipline and training | Having the most soldiers in the world | Magic shields from the gods | Fighting only at night in the dark
exercise: About how far did legionaries march in a day? | About 30 kilometres | About 3 kilometres | About 300 kilometres | About 3,000 kilometres
exercise: What did legionaries build every night on the march? | A fortified camp | A new road to Rome | A temple to Mars | A stone castle
exercise: How long did a legionary serve? | 25 years | 2 years | 50 years | 6 months
exercise: What was the testudo? | Soldiers locking shields like a tortoise shell | A Roman war elephant wearing heavy metal armour | A kind of short sword | A wooden siege tower
exercise: About how many kilometres of roads did the Romans build? | About 80,000 | About 800 | About 8 million | About 80
exercise: Why were Roman roads built straight and paved? | So armies and messengers could move fast | So that chariots could race each other along them | Because curves were against the law | To make the maps look nicer
exercise: What did aqueducts carry? | Fresh water to cities | Soldiers over rivers | Grain to ships | Messages between towns
exercise: How did aqueducts move water without pumps? | With a gentle downhill slope | With slaves carrying buckets | With wind and sails | With steam engines
exercise: Which Roman building still has the largest unreinforced concrete dome? | The Pantheon | The Colosseum | The Parthenon | Hadrian's Wall
exercise: About how long is Hadrian's Wall? | About 117 kilometres | About 11 kilometres | About 1,170 kilometres | About 11,700 kilometres
exercise: Who led a revolt against the Romans in Britain? | Boudica | Cleopatra | Hatshepsut | Joan of Arc
exercise: Which town did Boudica burn? | Londinium | Rome | Athens | Carthage
test: uncle says nero fiddled while rome burned. true? | No, the fiddle had not even been invented | Yes, he played a long violin solo from the palace roof | Yes, and the Senate clapped | No, he played the drums
test: A friend thinks all Roman soldiers came from Italy. What is true? | Soldiers came from all over the empire, even Iraq and North Africa | Every one of them was born in the city of Rome | Only Greeks were allowed to join the army | Soldiers came only from Britain
test: If Rome had no roads, what would be hardest? | Moving armies and messages quickly | Growing grapes for wine in the hills | Building houses and temples in the city | Speaking Latin to each other properly
test: Arrows rain down on a group of soldiers. Which Roman trick helps? | Locking shields into a tortoise | Running away in all directions as fast as possible | Lying down in a ditch | Throwing away their shields
test: Why do many roads in Europe today still follow Roman routes? | The Romans chose good straight lines and built them well | The Romans banned anyone from building new roads | Roads can never be moved, even a little | Roman roads were made of gold
test: Boudica is still remembered in Britain. Why? | As a symbol of courage against injustice | As the very first Roman emperor | As the inventor of roads | As the general who built Hadrian's Wall
test: What would happen to a city if its aqueduct broke? | It would run short of fresh water | It would get far too much water and flood the streets | Nothing, it rained every day | It would move to the hills
test: Someone says Romans were just copiers and invented nothing. What is a fair answer? | They made great use of arches, domes and concrete | They invented the wheel and fire long before anyone | They invented nothing useful at all | They invented the steam engine
test: so the legions got land or cash when they quit after 25 yrs? | Yes, and many settled in the provinces | No, they got nothing at all, not even thanks | They became kings | They had to go back to Rome forever
test: Why did Hadrian build a wall across Britain? | To mark the empire's edge and control movement | To keep the sea out of the farms in the north | To make a road to Scotland | To keep Romans in prison
test: In a strategy game you can only win with huge numbers. How did Romans often win instead? | With discipline, training and teamwork | With more soldiers than anyone | With dragons | By never fighting
exam: Identify the extent of the Roman Empire at its greatest dimensions. | From Britain to Egypt and from Spain to Syria | From Gaul in the west as far as China in the east | From Italy to Greece only | From Iceland to India
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the testudo? | A formation wherein shields were interlocked above and around the soldiers | A siege engine propelled by elephants | A type of short sword | An aqueduct of three tiers
exam: Ascertain the principle whereby the aqueducts conveyed water to the cities. | A gradual descending gradient | Mechanical pumps driven by steam | The labour of slaves bearing vessels | The force of the wind upon sails
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning Boudica. | A queen of the Iceni who led a revolt against Roman rule in Britain | The first empress of Rome, wife of Augustus | The architect of Hadrian's Wall | A general of Carthage who fought beside Hannibal against Rome
